    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Tests pass. **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification requires that all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nAfter inventorying every rejection path in the OfficeFloor escalation handlers, all of them already emitted proper `ProblemDetail` bodies via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (which sets `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`) \u2014 **except one**: the 400 path for missing/blank required fields.\n\n`RequiredFieldsExceptionHandler` was sending a plain `ValidationErrorsResponse` (`{\"errors\":[...]}`) as `application/json` with no `status`/`type`/`title`/`detail` members. This is exactly the path the acceptance test exercises (removing `city` \u2192 `RequiredFieldsException`).\n\n**Change made** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/escalation/RequiredFieldsExceptionHandler.java`:\n- Now builds a `ProblemDetail` via `ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, ex.getMessage())`, giving it the RFC7807 `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail` fields like every other handler.\n- Preserves the offending field names by attaching them as an `errors` property on the problem body, so no information is lost.\n\nThe `ValidationErrorsResponse` class is now unused; I left it in place since removing it isn't required by the spec, but it can be deleted if you'd prefer no dead code \u2014 let me know.",
